            {
              "type": "reasoning.summary",
              "summary": "**Analyzing confidence signals**\n\nI'm looking at how confidence values are used in reports. They might rely on peer reports for summaries and infer counts from the confidence levels. If all three have a Bayesian posterior of 0.771428, why is the confidence for mixed cases (.692307) different from what's expected (.6)? It seems the .6 could be intentionally cautious. Peer confidence could serve as a predictive measure of accuracy. To analyze effectively, averaging six equal analysts is useful for evaluating forecasts.**Calculating peer confidence**\n\nI’m identifying how peer confidence impacts probability estimates. For example, peer reports show values for A and B, leading to a sum of 2.8 when I add the individual contributions. Then averaging that gives me a result of about 0.4667. It raises the question of why we need a generative model if the goal is to compute my own forecast. I wonder if the intention is for equal weighting among analysts since no clear instruction exists for averaging.",
